# Target C-II — uniform strong-branch capacity certifies matched gap `0.0077`

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the matched all-train/rhombic atomic gap  
**Scope:** canonical full-core Type-II train-measure branch  
**Dependencies:** hostile-audited iterative-pruning physical pair theorem; hostile-audited corrected `g=0.0076` one-orbit capacity proof  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The corrected uniform strong-branch orbit-capacity theorem proves the matched atomic gap

\[
 g=0.0076
\]

and records a numerical architecture ceiling near `0.0077072732`.  The same proof has enough exact margin to certify the safely rounded value

\[
\boxed{g=0.0077.}
\tag{G77.1}
\]

Consequently, with

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\]

the proof-only train-measure floors become

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {T-q/2\over D}
\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0077,
}
\tag{G77.2}
\]

and, by rerunning the same theorem on the strict-rhombus train graph,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {T_R\over D}
\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0077,
\qquad
\liminf {\sigma_R\over D}
\ge0.0154.
}
\tag{G77.3}
\]

No geometric ingredient changes relative to the hostile-audited `0.0076` proof.  This note records the exact outward re-certification at `0.0077` and the resulting downstream constants.

---

## 1. Retained physical pair theorem

Put

\[
K={2\over\sqrt3},
\qquad
r_*:=\sqrt{20\over29}.
\]

For two exact train-phase atom families of masses `a,b<=K`, whose projective directions differ by `pi/3`, let `rho` be their normalized missing-pair mass.  The hostile-audited iterative-pruning theorem gives

\[
\rho\ge
\min\left\{
{(a+b-K)_+^2\over10},
{2\min(a,b)^2\over29}
\right\}.
\tag{G77.4}
\]

Whenever

\[
{(a+b-K)_+\over\min(a,b)}\le r_*,
\]

the strong branch applies:

\[
\boxed{
\rho\ge{(a+b-K)_+^2\over10}.
}
\tag{G77.5}
\]

The `0.0076` proof already establishes that the actual one-orbit capacity maximizer lies uniformly inside this strong branch; only the endpoint mass `A_0` changes below.

---

## 2. One-orbit capacity at `g=0.0077`

Write

\[
s:=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad
A_0:=2s+0.0077.
\tag{G77.6}
\]

Assume for contradiction that the total exact atomic train mass `A` satisfies `A<=A_0`.  Let `W` be a dominant exact `pi/3` orbit mass.  The generic orbit-square argument gives

\[
\boxed{
W\ge M_0:={4\Delta_2\over A_0}.
}
\tag{G77.7}
\]

For one orbit write its three phase masses as

\[
C\ge U\ge V\ge0,
\qquad
W=C+U+V.
\]

On the strong branch the center/leaf correlation and (G77.5) give the same physical capacity function as in the `0.0076` theorem:

\[
\boxed{
\Phi_{1/10}(W,C)
=C(W-C)
-{1\over10}{3C-W\over C}
\left({W+C\over2}-K\right)^2
}
\tag{G77.8}
\]

throughout the relevant optimizer interval `W/3<=C<=W/2`.  The function is strictly concave there, with

\[
\partial_C^2\Phi_{1/10}\le-2.
\tag{G77.9}
\]

Exactly as in the audited parent theorem, the strong/fallback boundary is

\[
C_s(W)=W-{W-K\over r_*}.
\tag{G77.10}
\]

and the fallback region is controlled by `Phi_{2/29}(W,C_s(W))` because the fallback capacity decreases for `C>=C_s(W)>W/2`.

The companion exact certificate checks all scope and monotonicity inequalities at `A_0`:

\[
M_0>1.89695,
\]

\[
2-{2K\over A_0}<0.79236<r_*,
\]

\[
\partial_C\Phi_{1/10}(W,W/3)>0.6254,
\]

and, after adding the generic residual-orbit square capacity, both the strong and fallback total capacities increase with `W`.  Therefore each branch is worst at

\[
W=A_0.
\tag{G77.11}
\]

---

## 3. Exact strong-capacity certificate

At `W=A_0` choose the rational tangent point

\[
c_0=0.935386.
\]

Strong concavity gives

\[
\max_C\Phi_{1/10}(A_0,C)
\le
\Phi_{1/10}(A_0,c_0)
+{\Phi_C(A_0,c_0)^2\over4}.
\tag{G77.12}
\]

The exact outward-Fraction verifier

`research/verify_target_c_uniform_strong_branch_orbit_capacity_gap_0077.py`

uses a Machin enclosure for `pi`, integer-square-root enclosures, and rational arithmetic only for load-bearing assertions.  It proves

\[
\boxed{
\Delta_2-\max_C\Phi_{1/10}(A_0,C)
>6.67\times10^{-6}.
}
\tag{G77.13}
\]

The fallback branch has much larger margin:

\[
\boxed{
\Delta_2-\Phi_{2/29}(A_0,C_s(A_0))
>1.39\times10^{-3}.
}
\tag{G77.14}
\]

Thus no atomic measure of total mass at most `A_0` can carry the required strict-rhombus crossing mass `Delta_2`.  This proves

\[
A>2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0077-o(1).
\tag{G77.15}
\]

The exposed-hull comparison `A<=(T-q/2)/D` proves (G77.2).  The strict-rhombus transfer used in the hostile-audited parent theorem is independent of singleton trains and gives (G77.3).

---

## 4. Scope and frontier

The gain from `0.0076` to `0.0077` is small but certified.  It is close to the numerical ceiling of the present one-orbit capacity architecture; the remaining numerical room is only about `7.3e-6` in `g`.  Further useful progress should therefore come from an additional geometric constraint on the single dominant exact orbit, not finer tuning of the same scalar optimizer.

A particularly relevant exact constraint, developed separately, is the one-shore train-projection capacity: for a phase class of mass `C` with opposite phase masses `U,V` and center/leaf missing masses `x,y`, source diameter gives

\[
{\sqrt3\over2}\,[C(U+V)-x-y]\le C.
\]

It is nonbinding at the present capacity maximizer, so it does not justify a value above `0.0077` by itself.

**Disposition:** proof-only matched atomic strengthening.  C-II and Level I remain open.
